---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import FriendlyBadge from '../components/FriendlyBadge.astro';
---
<Base title="Privacy — Ari" description="How Ari keeps your voice and your data on your device: private by default, cloud by choice.">
  <article class="privacy-page wrap">
    <header class="pp-hero">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
      <h1>Here's the deal with your data.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Most voice assistants ship your voice off to a data centre and ask you to trust the privacy policy. Ari mostly doesn't need to — the hard work happens right on your device. And on the rare occasion something does use the internet, Ari tells you. No small print, no surprises.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="pp-block">
      <h2>What happens on your device</h2>
      <p>Quite a lot, actually. When you say &ldquo;Hey Ari&rdquo;, the wake word is spotted on the phone. Your speech is turned into text on the phone. Working out what you actually meant? Phone. There's even a small language model on board for the open-ended questions, so Ari can answer plenty with no signal at all. None of that needs the internet to work.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-block">
      <h2>What leaves your device — and only when you say so</h2>
      <p>Here's the honest bit. A few things do use the network, but always because you asked them to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cloud assistants.</strong> Plug in ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini for the big questions and your question goes to that provider, with your own API key. Entirely optional.</li>
        <li><strong>Skills with a job to do.</strong> Asking for the weather or turning off the lights means talking to a weather service or your smart home. That's rather the point of them.</li>
        <li><strong>Maps, when you set up a place.</strong> Telling Ari to listen at a particular place shows you a map, and those tiles come from OpenFreeMap. Fetching them tells that service roughly which area you're looking at — and it opens where you are, so that's the first thing it sees. The place you save, and the fence Ari draws around it, stay on your phone.</li>
        <li><strong>Every skill is labelled.</strong> In the skills browser, each one is marked <em>on-device</em> or <em>uses-network</em> — so you know before you install, not after.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-block">
      <h2>No account. No telemetry. No kidding.</h2>
      <p>There's nothing to sign up for — no profile, no login for the core assistant. And there's no analytics quietly counting your taps, no crash-reporter phoning home, no &ldquo;anonymous usage data&rdquo;. We checked: there isn't an analytics SDK anywhere in the app, because we never put one there. The only time anything about your phone travels to us is a bug report you filled in and sent yourself — which is worth a section of its own.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-block">
      <!-- The one heading on this page with an id: ari-android's README and
           the in-app reporter both deep-link to it. Astro does not slugify
           headings in .astro the way it does in markdown, so without this
           the link lands on the page and goes nowhere. -->
      <h2 id="bug-reports">If you send us a bug report</h2>
      <p>Testing builds of Ari have a report button. It does nothing until you press it, and it shows you everything it is about to send before it sends it. There is no automatic crash upload — if Ari falls over, it saves the stack trace on your phone and asks you next time you open it.</p>
      <p>A report comes in two halves, and they are treated very differently.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The public half</strong> becomes a GitHub issue that anyone can read: what you typed in the description box, your phone model and Android version, Ari's version and settings, which skills you have installed, and the stack trace if it crashed. No files go in here. No screenshots, and never any audio.</li>
        <li><strong>The private half</strong> is the files you tick — a log, a screenshot, this session's conversation, voice recordings. Every one is a separate tick, and the ones with your voice in them start switched off. Underneath sits a consent box that also starts unticked. Leave it alone and the report still files perfectly well, just without the files.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Before the log goes anywhere it gets scrubbed on your phone: the API keys you have saved, the server addresses you configured, email addresses, phone numbers. The preview shows you the scrubbed version, not the original, so what you approve is what we get. Scrubbing is best-effort by its nature — which is exactly why the log goes in the private half and never the public issue.</p>
      <p>The files land in a locked-down bucket in our own AWS account in London, encrypted on the way there and encrypted at rest. That is not end-to-end encryption and we won't dress it up as such: Ari's maintainers can open those files, because reading them is the entire point. Nobody else can.</p>
      <p>They are deleted automatically 90 days later, and you can withdraw a report before then from Settings &rsaquo; Debug &rsaquo; My Reports. Two honest caveats about doing that:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The files really are gone.</strong> Those are ours to erase, and withdrawing erases them.</li>
        <li><strong>The issue is blanked, not deleted.</strong> GitHub keeps the edit history of an issue, so withdrawing takes your words out of view rather than out of the world. We would rather tell you that than let you assume otherwise.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The key that proves a report is yours is kept on your phone and nowhere else. That is deliberate — it means nobody else can withdraw your report — but it does mean that if you uninstall Ari, the 90-day deletion is what's left.</p>
      <p>The lawful basis for all of this is your consent, given by ticking that box, and you can withdraw it as described above. AWS processes the files on our behalf. GitHub hosts the public issue.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-block">
      <h2>Open all the way down</h2>
      <p>You don't have to believe a word of this. The engine, the apps, and the signed skills registry are all out in the open — read the code, poke at it, or fork the whole thing.</p>
      <p class="pp-links">
        <a href="https://github.com/ari-digital-assistant">GitHub org</a> ·
        <a href="https://github.com/ari-digital-assistant/ari-skills">ari-skills</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-block pp-manifesto">
      <div>
        <h2>We signed the Friendly Manifesto</h2>
        <p>A plain-language promise to build honest, secure, respectful software that doesn't exploit the people using it. That's the whole idea behind Ari, so signing up was easy.</p>
      </div>
      <FriendlyBadge />
    </section>

    <footer class="pp-close">
      <p>That's the deal.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#get">Get Ari</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/ari-digital-assistant">Read the code</a>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>
<style>
  /* Scoped page styles, consistent with the Dusk system. Shared primitives
     (.wrap, .eyebrow, .btn/.btn-primary/.btn-ghost) come from global.css and
     are NOT redefined here. Everything below uses tokens so both themes hold. */

  /* .wrap gives the 1120px column; add page rhythm and a narrower prose
     measure so running text stays around 65ch. */
  .privacy-page{padding-block:60px 96px}

  /* Hero — big display scale like the home hero (Manrope ExtraBold + tight
     tracking inherited from global h1); muted, larger lede. */
  .pp-hero{max-width:62ch}
  .pp-hero h1{font-size:clamp(40px,6vw,64px); line-height:1.03; margin-top:16px}
  .lede{
    margin-top:22px; font-size:clamp(17px,2vw,20px); line-height:1.5;
    color:var(--muted); max-width:56ch; font-weight:450;
  }

  /* Blocks — reset the global section 72px padding to a tighter margin rhythm;
     keep prose ~65ch for readability. */
  .pp-block{padding:0; margin-block:44px; max-width:65ch}
  .pp-block h2{font-size:clamp(24px,3vw,32px); line-height:1.12; margin-bottom:14px}
  .pp-block p{color:var(--ink)}
  .pp-block p + p{margin-top:14px}

  .pp-block ul{margin:18px 0 0; padding-left:1.15em; line-height:1.7}
  .pp-block li{margin-bottom:12px}
  .pp-block li:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
  .pp-block li strong{color:var(--ink); font-weight:700}
  /* The on-device / uses-network labels read as the same chips users meet in
     the skills browser — mono, bordered, token-coloured. */
  .pp-block li em{
    font-style:normal; font-family:var(--mono); font-size:.82em;
    letter-spacing:.02em; padding:1px 7px; border-radius:6px;
    background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--hair); color:var(--ink);
    white-space:nowrap;
  }

  .pp-links{margin-top:16px; font-family:var(--mono); font-size:14px; color:var(--muted)}
  .pp-links a{color:var(--sun); font-weight:500}
  .pp-links a:hover{text-decoration:underline; text-underline-offset:3px}

  /* Manifesto — a card that puts the copy and the badge side by side, and
     stacks on narrow screens. */
  .pp-manifesto{
    max-width:840px; margin-block:52px;
    display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; gap:32px;
    background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--hair);
    border-radius:16px; padding:28px 32px; box-shadow:var(--shadow);
  }
  .pp-manifesto > div{max-width:52ch}
  .pp-manifesto h2{margin-bottom:12px}
  .pp-manifesto p{color:var(--muted); font-size:16px}
  .pp-manifesto :global(.friendly){flex:none}

  /* Close — a firm sign-off and the shared buttons. */
  .pp-close{margin-top:60px}
  .pp-close p{
    font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,30px); font-weight:800; letter-spacing:-.02em;
    color:var(--ink);
  }
  .pp-close .cta-row{display:flex; gap:14px; margin-top:22px; flex-wrap:wrap}

  @media (max-width:640px){
    .pp-manifesto{flex-direction:column; align-items:flex-start; gap:24px}
    .pp-manifesto > div{max-width:none}
  }
</style>
